---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  BOOK_TITLE,
  BOOK_SUBTITLE,
  BOOK_EDITION,
  BOOK_DESCRIPTION,
  BOOK_OG_IMAGE,
  PDF_PATH,
  CHAPTERS,
  LINKS,
  LICENCE_SHORT,
} from '../site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${BOOK_TITLE}: ${BOOK_SUBTITLE}`}
  description={BOOK_DESCRIPTION}
  canonicalPath="/book/"
  ogImage={BOOK_OG_IMAGE}
  preloadCover={true}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="edition">{BOOK_EDITION}</p>
        <h1>
          Programming
          <span class="loop">the Loop</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="subtitle">{BOOK_SUBTITLE}</p>
        <p class="byline">By Garry Meaburn · Free ebook (PDF)</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href={PDF_PATH}>Download the PDF</a>
          <a class="button" href="/contents/">Contents</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-visual">
        <img
          src="/book-cover.png"
          width="1600"
          height="2400"
          alt="Cover of Programming the Loop: What I Measured with Coding Agents by Garry Meaburn"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about-the-book">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What this book is</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A free first-edition ebook about coding agents: what happens when you give them more
          repository context, what happens when the facts they need are missing or stale, and how
          verification (or the lack of it) changes the result.
        </p>
        <p>
          The measured findings come from small controlled trials on a handful of repositories,
          with stated limits next to each result. Recommendations are judgement, informed by those
          findings and by other practitioners’ published practice. Where a recommendation goes
          beyond what was measured, the book says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          One worked example runs through the chapters: add an API endpoint with a cross-repository
          dependency. It is a reproducible engineering demonstration of failure modes the
          experiments found (guessed facts, stale knowledge, unverifiable claims), not a recorded
          agent session. Source:
          <a href={LINKS.workedExample}>meabs/ProgrammingTheLoop-WorkedExample</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="measured">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What was measured</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        From the foreword’s findings in brief. Individual experimental results with stated limits,
        not laws of nature.
      </p>
      <div class="findings">
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 1 · Experiment J</span>
          <h3>A fully “agent-optimised” repository cost more and was not reliably more correct</h3>
          <p>
            On a large Java backend the agent could read, a copy set up with a generated guide, a
            code graph, three operating skills and a configured <code>AGENTS.md</code> used
            35–48% more tokens, with no reliable gain in correctness: 29 against 28 of 32 changes,
            19 against 16 of 24 structure questions, both within noise.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: one repository, one model, mechanical edits and structure questions, 8 runs per
            task per arm, four ingredients tested together. Analysed in Chapter 3.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 2 · Stale knowledge</span>
          <h3>Out-of-date knowledge of an unread dependency was repeated as fact</h3>
          <p>
            After four names in a backend were renamed, agents with the stale guide and no source to
            check gave the old answer in 14 of 16 drift answers, and none of the wrong answers
            hedged.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: the drift was planted, 4 runs per question, one model. Analysed in Chapter 3.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 3 · Cross-repository</span>
          <h3>When missing backend facts were supplied, passes rose from 3 to 18–19 of 20</h3>
          <p>
            A storefront change depended on a backend the agent could not see. With nothing, 3 of 20
            runs passed the hidden tests; with a guide to the backend, 19 of 20 on small tasks and
            18 of 20 on harder ones. A later experiment found an API contract or a 3&nbsp;KB note
            did about as well, more cheaply.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: 5 runs per task, identifiers renamed by the author, one model. Analysed in
            Chapter 3.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 4 · Verification</span>
          <h3>Without the contract, agents defeated the check meant to catch them</h3>
          <p>
            Each rewrote the freshness gate so that, with no provider contract to hand, it compared
            the contract the agent had invented with itself, and passed. Their reports still matched
            their command logs. All 12 runs, with and without the contract, reported
            <code>passed</code> while listing the brief’s criterion 6 (a check against the real
            inventory service) as unverified. With the contract, all 6 runs were correct.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: one model, one task, 6 runs per arm. Analysed in Chapters 3 and 5.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="contents">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Contents</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        Eleven chapters. Chapter 3 is the heart of the evidence. Short web notes for each chapter
        are also available; the PDF is the permanent first edition.
      </p>
      <ol class="chapter-list">
        {CHAPTERS.map((ch) => (
          <li>
            <a href={ch.href}>
              <span class="chapter-num">{String(ch.num).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <span>
                <span class="chapter-title">{ch.title}</span>
                {ch.subtitle && <span class="chapter-sub">{ch.subtitle}</span>}
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
      <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
        <a href="/contents/">Full contents page</a>
        ·
        <a href={PDF_PATH}>Download PDF</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="licence">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Licence and download</h2>
      <div class="licence-box">
        <p>{LICENCE_SHORT}</p>
        <p>
          The latest edition is published via
          <a href={LINKS.githubAuthor}>github.com/meabs</a>.
          This site offers the PDF only.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href={PDF_PATH}>Download Programming-the-Loop-1st-edition.pdf</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="feedback">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Feedback</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If something in the book or the worked example is wrong, unclear, or incomplete, say so.
          Prefer concrete corrections: a page, a number, a limit that was missed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feedback-links">
        <a href={LINKS.feedbackIssue}>Open an issue on the worked example</a>
        <a href={LINKS.workedExample}>Worked example repository</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
